# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in the values from your Supabase project.
# Supabase Dashboard -> your project -> Project Settings -> API
#
# These are PUBLIC, browser-safe values only.
# NEVER put the service_role / secret key in this file or anywhere else in frontend code.

VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-PROJECT-REF.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=YOUR-PUBLISHABLE-KEY

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google sign-in (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase Dashboard -> Authentication -> Providers -> Google: enable it and
# paste in the client id and secret from Google Cloud Console -> APIs & Services
# -> Credentials.
#
# Then add ALL of these to Authentication -> URL Configuration -> Redirect URLs,
# for every origin you deploy to (including http://localhost:5173):
#   https://your-app.example.com/auth/callback    (Google / OAuth return, web)
#   https://your-app.example.com/reset-password    (password recovery email, web)
#   com.ridetogether.app:/auth/callback            (Google / OAuth return, Android)
#
# THE ANDROID LINE IS NOT OPTIONAL. The app is a Capacitor build, so it asks
# Supabase to return to its own scheme rather than to a web page. Supabase
# silently ignores a `redirect_to` it does not recognise and substitutes the
# project's Site URL instead, so with that line missing a member picks their
# Google account and is then dropped on http://localhost:3000 with no session and
# no error - which looks exactly like a broken app rather than a missing setting.
# Note the single slash: a custom scheme has no host, so it is `scheme:/path`.
#
# Leave VITE_AUTH_REDIRECT_URL unset to use the deployment's own origin, which is
# what you want for local development. The app appends /auth/callback itself, so
# this variable is only needed when the browser starts the flow on a different
# origin than it should return to. If you do set it, give the FULL callback URL
# including /auth/callback - it is used verbatim. It is ignored on Android, which
# always uses the scheme above, so one build can serve web and native at once.
# VITE_AUTH_REDIRECT_URL=https://your-app.example.com/auth/callback

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Web Push (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is the ONLY push value the browser ever reads, and it is public by
# design. The base64url encoding of an UNCOMPRESSED P-256 point: 0x04 followed
# by the 32-byte x and y coordinates (65 bytes total). Generate a matching key
# pair with:
#   deno run --allow-env supabase/functions/send-push/generate-vapid.ts
# Do not substitute the x coordinate on its own - the browser rejects a 32-byte
# applicationServerKey, so every subscription would silently fail.
#
# NEVER add the private key, the x/y JWK coordinates, the function URL or the
# shared dispatch secret to this file, to any other VITE_ variable, or to
# Cloudflare's frontend environment. Those live in `supabase secrets set` and in
# Vault; see supabase/README.md for the full split. A VITE_ variable is compiled
# into the JavaScript bundle and is readable by anyone who opens devtools.
#
# With this unset, push is simply unavailable and the app says so; the in-app
# notification list is unaffected.
# VITE_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=BEl62iUYgUivxIkv69yViEuiBIa-Ib9-SkvMeAtA3LFgDzkrxZBBSg4t4jYqxsb1jP27JrVEPFWSYO7Sm3vw
